WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.500
Hey, this is Presh Talwalkar and you're watching Mind Your Decisions

00:00:05.529 --> 00:00:07.559
A, B, and C have a party

00:00:08.559 --> 00:00:10.739
Person A brings five cakes

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:13.769
Person B brings three cakes

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.489
Person C brings eight dollars

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:26.999
A, B, and C believe in sharing. They divide the cakes so everyone eats the same amount

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:34.300
Person C then splits the eight dollars to A and B fairly

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:38.309
How much should C pay to A and to B?

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:46.500
Now before I go on I want to mention a very popular educational video on YouTube begins by asking

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:49.320
Who doesn't like birthday cake?

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:54.600
I get that many people like cake and I use cake in my math problems, too

00:00:55.390 --> 00:01:00.059
But as someone who does not eat birthday cake and does not regularly eat cake

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:05.789
I want to suggest maybe we can encourage eating healthier foods in our math problems?

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:12.839
So I originally heard this story told which chapatis a whole wheat unleavened flatbread

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.570
And I personally enjoy eating tortillas

00:01:16.119 --> 00:01:20.729
In particular white corn tortillas are inexpensive and easily available to me

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:24.299
So let's encourage these healthier foods in our math problems

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:31.379
Furthermore my videos will make you so smart that even difficult problems will become easy to you.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:35.579
In videos like this you'll see people requesting harder problems

00:01:35.579 --> 00:01:39.989
And that's the best proof that these videos are teaching math to people around the world

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:43.110
Can you figure it out?

00:01:43.299 --> 00:01:47.699
Give this problem a try and when you're ready keep watching the video for a solution

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:08.750
So person A brings five tortillas person B brings three tortillas and person C brings $8

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.009
Since there are eight tortillas and C has eight dollars

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:21.050
It seems like C should give one dollar per tortilla meaning five dollars to A and three dollars to B

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:27.499
But in fact the fair split is seven dollars to A and one dollar to B

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.320
Why is that?

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:35.540
Well to divide into three people. Let's cut each tortilla into three parts

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:44.389
Now we can count the number of parts and we'll see there are eight times three which equals twenty-four pieces in total

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:46.640
so each person should get

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:51.320
24 over 3 which equals 8 of these pieces

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:57.120
In order for the split to happen A needs to give seven pieces to C and

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.240
B needs to give one piece to C

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:06.019
So now you can see that every single person has the exact same amount of food

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:15.140
C is giving eight dollars for a total of eight pieces, which means each piece is worth one dollar

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:20.240
Consequently C should pay seven dollars to A

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:25.320
for those seven pieces and one dollar to B for that one piece

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:28.420
Did you figure it out?

